  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThis strategy deploys a structured random initialization combined with domain-specific optimization to minimize C\u2085. Unlike previous cluster-based methods, it uses a hybrid approach where the initial h is formed by placing active points (h[x] = 1) at random but equally spaced locations to balance overlaps. The optimization then refines this by applying conjugate gradient descent while maintaining the integral constraint. This addresses the bottleneck of previous methods by reducing the likelihood of getting trapped in local minima through diverse initialization and more thorough exploration.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe function h(x) is represented as a discrete vector of length n_points over [0, 2]. Let h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points, with dx = 2/n_points and \u2211h[i] = n_points/2 ensuring \u222bh = 1. The objective is to minimize the maximum overlap integral C\u2085 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx. This evaluates the cross-correlation of h with (1-h) across all lags k, scaled by dx. The constraints are:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h[i] == n_points/2\n\nThe optimization seeks to minimize the max of the discrete correlation values computed via np.correlate with mode=\"full\".\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Parent validation: If initial_h_values is provided, use it directly; otherwise, generate a random initial configuration.\nb. Initialization:\n   i. For n_points = 400, generate 200 positions uniformly in [0, 2] as x_i = dx * r_i, where r_i ~ Uniform(0, 1)\n   ii. Set h[i] = 1 if x_i is selected, 0 otherwise\n   iii. Normalize by scaling h to ensure \u2211h == n_points/2\nc. Optimization:\n   i. Define the objective function to return max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\") * dx)\n   ii. Use the SLSQP solver with bounds [0, 1] for all h[i]\n   iii. Add a hard constraint \u2211h[i] == n_points/2\n   iv. Perform 500 iterations of gradient descent, with 10% random perturbations to escape local optima\n   v. After each iteration, clip and normalize h to ensure constraints\nd. Candidate acceptance: Track the best solution during the optimization process\ne. Termination: Stop after 500 iterations or when the objective improves for 50 consecutive iterations\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\na. Default configuration:\n   - n_points = 400\n   - Random seed = 42\n   - Perturbation strength = 0.05\n   - Maximum iterations = 500\n   - Perturbation frequency = 50 iterations\n\nb. Variations:\n   1. Different initialization: Use a grid-based or Poisson process for initial placement\n   2. Varying perturbation magnitude (0.02 to 0.1) for exploration\n   3. Adjust n_points to 600 for higher resolution\n   4. Switch to COBYLA solver for constrained problems\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\na. Time allocation:\n   - 95% of time (570s) to the optimization loop\n   - 5% (30s) to perturbation and recovery phases\n\nb. Early termination checks:\n   - Monitor for stagnation (no improvement for 50 iterations)\n   - Stop early if time runs out, return the best solution\n\nc. Robustness measures:\n   - Fallback to the initial solution if optimization fails\n   - Ensure all h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] through clipping and normalization\n   - Validate \u2211h == n_points/2 after each iteration\n   - If numerical instability occurs, reduce perturbation magnitude and restart\n\n6. Validation and return contract\na. Feasibility checks:\n   - Verify all h[i] \u2208 [0, 1]\n   - Confirm \u2211h == n_points/2\n   - Ensure the correlation computation matches the evaluator's method\n\nb. Objective validation:\n   - Re-evaluate the best solution using the provided evaluator\n   - Compare with previous best values to ensure improvement\n\nc. Interface requirements:\n   - Return h_values as a list of floats\n   - Provide c5_bound as the computed maximum overlap\n   - Include n_points as the number of samples",
